<LegalLayout
  lang="en"
  title="Getting trail problem reports from your visitors"
  description="How a club, park or municipality can hear from its visitors that a tree is down or a trail is washed out — with no account, no app, and even with no signal."
>
  <p>
    A tree across the trail, a culvert washed away, a sign torn off: most of the time the crew finds out at
    the next patrol, or from a comment on a Facebook page. In between, dozens of visitors walked past the
    problem with no simple way to tell the people responsible.
  </p>

  <h2>It starts with a QR code at the trailhead</h2>
  <p>
    You print the stickers yourself from the steward desk: a print-ready sheet sized for adhesive vinyl, one
    code per trailhead. That is the whole install — no kiosk, no hardware, no visitor training.
  </p>
  <p>
    The visitor scans the code: the map opens in their browser, where they are standing. They pick a
    category, add a photo if they want, and the GPS position is captured on its own. No account, no app to
    install. If GPS will not answer, they place the point by hand. Photos are scrubbed of their hidden
    metadata (position, device) before they even leave the phone.
  </p>

  <h2>No signal, nothing lost</h2>
  <p>
    In the woods, signal is often missing. The report is then kept on the phone and sends itself when the
    network comes back. A report is never lost, and never sent twice.
  </p>

  <h2>What your crew gets</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A triage queue, not an inbox.</strong> Every report lands on your territory's map, with its
      position and, more often than not, a photo. Confirm it, fix it or reject it, and the history stays.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fewer duplicates.</strong> A second visitor who sees the same problem confirms it rather than
      reporting it again.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your own words.</strong> The report menu is yours: add your own categories, in French and
      English, on top of the defaults.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An email digest.</strong> An hourly digest keeps managers and owners in the loop.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What if nobody runs this land yet?</h2>
  <p>
    The visitor's position finds who answers for the land. If nobody does yet, the report waits: the day your
    organisation claims that territory and the claim is verified, the reports standing on it come to you.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>
  <p>
    Claim your land by drawing it on the map: you triage the reports on your ground from day one. Once the
    claim is verified, print a code for every trailhead. The free tier has no end date.
  </p>
  <p><a href={claimUrl}>Claim your land</a> · <a href="/en/pricing/">See pricing</a></p>
</LegalLayout>
